+ * ===========================================================================================
+ * Root classification for condemned collectible contexts (stats kinds 11-19)
+ * ===========================================================================================
+ *
+ * WHY. A downstream WASM host doing repeated collectible-ALC load/unload cycles observes the
+ * teardown reporting NotQuiescent forever: some root keeps the LoaderAllocator witnesses
+ * alive, and an exhaustive managed-side root trace found nothing — which leaves the roots
+ * managed reflection CANNOT enumerate (raw GCHandles, thread stacks/registers, native
+ * structures). These kinds narrow that down without a debugger or a heap dump.
+ *
+ * WHAT IS MEASURED — and what it does NOT prove. For every live GC handle, the target object
+ * is attributed to the condemned set (collectible + unloading contexts) in one of two ways:
+ *
+ *   CLASS attribution (kinds 11-14, bucketed by handle type): the TARGET's class belongs to
+ *   one of the condemned memory managers.
+ *
+ *   IDENTITY attribution (kind 19, class-blind): the TARGET *is* one of the condemned
+ *   contexts' own runtime objects — the managed AssemblyLoadContext wrapper (which
+ *   PrepareForAssemblyLoadContextRelease deliberately promotes to a STRONG handle while an
+ *   unload is in flight) or a manager's LoaderAllocator witness. Those objects are
+ *   corelib-classed, so class attribution is blind to them by construction; counting them
+ *   separately keeps the runtime's own expected roots distinguishable from third-party ones.
+ *
+ * Attribution is PER HANDLE and NON-TRANSITIVE. A non-zero bucket therefore establishes
+ * "a GC handle DIRECTLY TARGETS an object attributable to the condemned set" — a NECESSARY,
+ * not sufficient, condition for "a GC handle is the holder". All-zero buckets do NOT establish
+ * that no GC handle is involved: rooting is transitive, and a handle whose immediate target is
+ * corelib-classed while its object graph reaches a condemned instance lands in NO bucket. The
+ * miss classes, enumerated so nobody has to rediscover them:
+ *
+ *   - containers: object[] / List<object> / Dictionary<,> / boxes holding app instances;
+ *   - the reflection-cache family: RuntimeType, RuntimeAssembly, MethodInfo/FieldInfo — all
+ *     corelib-classed even when they DESCRIBE collectible metadata (this is where a real
+ *     residual root was already found downstream);
+ *   - delegates: Action/Func are corelib types; the target instance and method are not;
+ *   - pinned byte[]/T[] buffers held for interop (element class corelib);
+ *   - a strong handle placed directly on a LoaderAllocator that kind 16 counts as live, unless
+ *     it happens to be one of the identity objects kind 19 covers;
+ *   - anything reached only through native structures or thread stacks/registers.
+ *
+ * The sibling lifecycle test ForcedUnload_RootedPointerFreeObjects_BlockNativeFree already
